@page "/"

<PageTitle>ESIR – Your little companion</PageTitle>

<section class="hero bleed">
    <div class="container hero-inner">
        <div class="hero-text">
            <p class="hello">Hi, I'm ESIR!</p>
            <h1>Your little companion, always by your side.</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                I'm a small desk robot with a friendly face. Tell me how you feel and I'll answer with
                a kind word and a smile. If everything feels too fast, we can breathe slowly together.
            </p>
            <p class="actions">
                <a class="btn" href="relax">Tell me how you feel</a>
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-face">
            <RobotFace Mood="happy" Size="21px" />
            <p class="hint">Psst, click my face!</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<nav class="jump" aria-label="On this page">
    <span>On this page:</span>
    <a href="#reactions">How I react</a>
    <a href="#feelings">Six feelings</a>
    <a href="#inside">Inside the robot</a>
    <a href="#body">The body</a>
    <a href="#safe">Safe by design</a>
    <a href="#questions">Questions</a>
</nav>

<section>
    <h2>What ESIR is</h2>
    <p>
        ESIR stands for <strong>Emotional Supportive Interactive Robot</strong>. It is a small companion for your desk,
        for people who would like some company, someone to be there with a feeling, or a little help to slow down
        in a hard moment. You touch it, tilt it, shake it gently or pick it up, and it answers with an animated face
        and coloured light. Next to the robot there is this website, where you can tell ESIR how you feel and try
        exercises that help you relax.
    </p>
    <p>ESIR is for adults and young people, not only for students. It is not made for children.</p>
    <p class="stage">
        <strong>Where we are:</strong> ESIR is a student project at Mercantec, at the concept stage. The website works today.
        Everything about the robot on this page is planned, and the robot is not built yet.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>A moment with ESIR</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
        @foreach (var step in _day)
        {
            <li class="step">
                <RobotFace Mood="@step.Mood" Size="5px" />
                <h3>@step.Title</h3>
                <p>@step.Text</p>
            </li>
        }
    </ol>
</section>

<section id="reactions">
    <h2>How ESIR reacts to you</h2>
    <p>ESIR has touch pads and a motion sensor. This is what each action should do. We have not tuned any of it on the real robot yet.</p>
    <div class="reactions">
        @foreach (var r in _reactions)
        {
            <article class="reaction">
                <RobotFace Mood="@r.Mood" Size="4.5px" />
                <h3>@r.Trigger</h3>
                <p>@r.What</p>
                <p class="sensor">@r.Sensor</p>
            </article>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section id="feelings">
    <h2>Six feelings, two answers</h2>
    <p>
        When you pick a feeling, ESIR answers twice: on the robot and on the website. Today the website shows ESIR's face
        and a kind message. The rest of the website answers, and everything on the robot, are still being built.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Feeling</th><th scope="col">On the robot (planned)</th><th scope="col">On the website</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in _feelings)
                {
                    <tr><th scope="row">@row.Feeling</th><td>@row.Robot</td><td>@row.Website</td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="inside">
    <h2>Inside the robot</h2>
    <div class="facts">
        <div><h3>The brain</h3><p>An Arduino MKR WiFi 1010, a small board with Wi-Fi. It runs our program, written in Arduino C++.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The carrier</h3><p>An Arduino Opla IoT Carrier sits on the board. It brings the touch pads, a motion sensor, a round display and RGB lights.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The program</h3><p>Four parts: input, behaviour, drawing the face, and the network. It never waits, so ESIR always stays responsive.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The power</h3><p>Steady USB power. If power is low, ESIR goes into a safe low-power mode, and after a restart it starts quietly with no lights or sounds.</p></div>
    </div>
    <h3 class="states-title">ESIR is always in one of five states</h3>
    <ul class="states">
        @foreach (var s in _states)
        {
            <li>@s</li>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="body">
    <h2>The body</h2>
    <p>The board sits inside a 3D-printed shell that we design in CAD. Both ideas below are concepts, and the fit still has to be checked.</p>
    <div class="two">
        <div class="box box--now">
            <h3>Version 1: a fixed disc</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>A flat disc shell, with the board between two halves.</li>
                <li>A window for the display.</li>
                <li>Open touch pads, so you can reach them.</li>
                <li>USB access and a removable back.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box box--soon">
            <h3>Version 3 idea: a body that moves</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>A head and arms moved by small motors (servos), on the same board mount.</li>
                <li>We would start with one head movement only.</li>
                <li>This only happens if there is time.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="concepts">
        <img src="images/esir-concepts.jpg" alt="Four ESIR enclosure concepts: a mint cat, a classic blue robot, a peach box and a lilac pebble." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Four shell concepts to choose from: a mint cat, a classic robot, a peach box and a lilac pebble. Board size, touch openings and fit still need to be checked.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section id="safe">
    <h2>Safe by design</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
        <li><strong>Stop always wins.</strong> It cancels whatever ESIR is doing and returns it to quiet rest. You can stop it with a touch pad or from the website.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits on lights and sound,</strong> with a way to switch them off.</li>
        <li><strong>No surprises after a restart.</strong> ESIR starts with no lights or sounds on.</li>
        <li><strong>Old commands are ignored.</strong> Every command has an ID, so a repeated or outdated command is skipped.</li>
        <li><strong>It should keep working without Wi-Fi,</strong> and the website only shows what the robot has confirmed.</li>
        <li><strong>No recordings and no personal data.</strong> You can't talk to ESIR directly yet. A microphone is only a later idea, and only after privacy checks.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>What works today, and what comes later</h2>
    <div class="two">
        <div class="box box--now">
            <h3>On the website today</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>On the Relax page you pick one of the six feelings. ESIR answers with a face and a kind message, and the feeling is queued for the robot.</li>
                <li>The same page has three breathing rhythms, a 5-4-3-2-1 grounding exercise and a body release exercise.</li>
                <li>A status line shows whether the robot is online.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box box--soon">
            <h3>Ideas for later versions</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Version 2:</strong> a name, eye colour and brightness you can choose, finer gestures, and testing both versions of the Opla carrier.</li>
                <li><strong>Version 3:</strong> a moving head, a microphone after privacy checks, voice replies through a laptop, more sensors and games, and ESIRs you can customise.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>How the robot and the website talk</h2>
    <ol class="flow">
        <li>You pick a feeling on the website. The website puts a command for the robot in a waiting line on the server.</li>
        <li>About once a second the robot asks the server: "Is there something for me?" The server never calls the robot.</li>
        <li>The robot shows the matching reaction and tells the server each step, with the command ID and the status: received, running, completed.</li>
        <li>The website shows only what the robot has confirmed. If the robot is offline, the command waits for a short while and then expires.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>There is no database in the first version. Commands and robot status live in memory, and nothing personal is stored.</p>
    <div class="facts">
        <div><h3>The website</h3><p>ASP.NET Core on .NET 10 with Blazor Server, and a small API that the robot talks to over Wi-Fi.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The firmware</h3><p>Arduino C++, built with PlatformIO.</p></div>
        <div><h3>The tools</h3><p>GitHub for tasks and code, Microsoft Teams for talking, and CAD for the body. Hosting with Docker is planned.</p></div>
    </div>
</section>